WHAT: when several stories finish together, one tick starts every grade at once, and each grade runs the full suite with `-n auto` (pyproject.toml addopts), so they fan out to every core. Observed 2026-10-30: three grades plus two agent suites ran ~70 xdist workers on 10 cores (load average ~50) and a 2-minute suite took over 13 minutes. Nothing bounds the count: `pipeline/detached_grade.py` `_detached_grade_lifecycle` spawns through `starter(...)` whenever an agent has finished.

CHANGE (pipeline/detached_grade.py only, 343 lines; pipeline/story_status.py is at 987 lines and must not grow):
- `max_concurrent_grades(environ=None) -> int`: `PIPELINE_MAX_CONCURRENT_GRADES`, default 1; a value that is not a positive integer (0, -1, "x", "") falls back to 1 with a WARN naming the variable and the bad value (once per distinct value).
- `live_grade_count(state_root: Path) -> int`: count stories across every `*.manifest.json` directly under the manifest directory (the parent of this story's manifest path; same base the grading channel uses) whose `grading_pid` is a live, non-zombie process (reuse the same os.kill(pid, 0) + `ps -o stat=` probe the lifecycle already uses - factor it into a small helper and call it from both places). Unreadable/malformed manifests are skipped. Do not count the current story.
- In `_detached_grade_lifecycle`, in the branch where `grading_pid is None` and the starter is reachable, right before computing the grading channel: if `live_grade_count(...) >= max_concurrent_grades()`, do NOT start a grade; leave the story untouched (`in_progress`, no grading fields, no manifest write) and return `({"status": "grading_queued", "pid": pid}, None)`. Check how the caller (pipeline/story_status.py check_story_status) treats the first element's status; if an unknown status string would be mis-handled, return the same tuple shape the `elif grading_alive:` branch returns instead and say so in a comment. The scratchpad untrack step that runs before may still run each tick (it is idempotent) - verify that it is, by reading `_untrack_scratchpad`.
- `PIPELINE_GRADE_XDIST_WORKERS` (optional): when set to a positive integer and `test_cmd` invokes pytest (an element ending in "pytest", or "-m" followed by "pytest"), append `["-n", "<value>"]` to the command passed to `starter` (a later -n overrides addopts). Invalid value -> ignored with a WARN. Unset -> command unchanged.

VALIDATE ON THE REAL HOST (required, `.claude/rules/testing-config-gates.md`): after the tests pass, time `.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q` once plainly and once with `-n 4`, and record in the PR description both wall times, the core count, and the chosen defaults. Default stays cap 1 / workers unset unless the measurement says otherwise.

